 "transcript": {
  "answer": "The 10-year government bond yield is well above the cash rate, by about 0.95 percentage points.\n\n- **10-year bond yield:** 5.31% on 14 October 2026, the latest observation. It has risen from 4.44% on 2 January 2025 and from a low of 4.09% on 17 October 2025.\n- **Cash rate target:** 4.36% as a September 2026 monthly average, the latest figure available. It was 4.35% in January 2025, fell to 3.60% from late 2025 to January 2026, and has since risen back to about 4.35%.\n\nThe gap has widened over the past year. In January 2025 the 10-year yield was only about 0.1 points above the cash rate. By December 2025 it was about 1.1 points above (4.74% against 3.60%). It has since settled at roughly 0.9 to 1.0 points.\n\nA 10-year yield well above the cash rate is the normal shape for a yield curve. Markets are pricing in higher short-term rates over the coming decade, or a term premium, rather than expecting cuts. The cash rate figure is a monthly average, so it may not reflect any change made in October 2026.",
